Title:
Blackbird — Kernel Malware Investigation Platform / Real-time Malware Analysis Suite & IDS

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Blackbird is a Windows-focused, VM-oriented malware analysis and reverse-engineering platform that provides real-time, instrumentable telemetry with a local analysis UI.  
- It solves the problem of collecting high-fidelity behavioral data during detonation/triage by combining kernel-level capture with in-process usermode telemetry and offline session review.  
- The project emphasizes deep Windows instrumentation: process/thread/image/handle/memory/registry/filesystem/network capture, thread stack visibility, ETW/COM/performance views, and execution control (suspended launch, deferred resume, attach workflows).  
- Captures can be archived as `.bkcap` (SQLite + LZ4) and detections exported to common SOC pipelines (JSONL, Splunk HEC JSON, Elastic ECS NDJSON, CEF, CSV).  
- It’s primarily useful for malware analysts, DFIR, threat hunters, and blue teams building detections; red teams can use it to validate behavioral OPSEC and understand detection surfaces.  
- Interesting due to its kernel driver component (KMDF) and end-to-end workflow from detonation to exportable detection artifacts, with explicit warnings about stability and VM-only usage.

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel instrumentation (KMDF driver)
- Behavioral telemetry capture (process/thread/image/handle/memory/registry/file/network)
- Usermode in-process telemetry + execution control (launch/attach/suspended start)
- ETW/COM/performance correlation and thread stack analysis
- Offline capture format and analysis pipeline (`.bkcap` SQLite + LZ4)
- SIEM-friendly detection export (ECS, Splunk HEC, CEF, CSV)

Use Cases:
- Detonate and triage suspicious EXEs/DLLs with kernel + usermode visibility
- Build and validate behavioral detections from captured activity, then export to SIEM
- Investigate process trees, child process spawning, handle usage, and memory mappings
- Analyze thread stacks and timing behavior (QPC compensation) during execution
- Perform offline review/sharing of capture sessions for DFIR workflows

Keywords:
Windows 10 22H2, kernel driver, KMDF, WDK, Visual Studio 2022, malware detonation, dynamic analysis, kernel telemetry, process monitoring, thread monitoring, image load, handle operations, memory mapping, registry monitoring, filesystem monitoring, network monitoring, ETW, COM, thread stacks, QPC, SQLite, LZ4, Splunk HEC, Elastic ECS, CEF, IDS, DFIR, threat hunting